A||||5||mrfixit58||Power Antenna in the Stock Location||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||02-03-2000||05:25 PM||||I used to belong to that "other" Fiero chat group and at one time there was someone who discussed installing a power antenna in the stock location. Unfortunatly, the note containing the instructions was lost on my last computer crash (bad boy, didn't back up). Anyway, any of you out there know of this conversion or performed it? If so, can you lay out the procedures for the rest of us??<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy L.||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||02-03-2000||05:40 PM||||I'd be interested too. I have a spare GM power antenna I would like to add in the stock location.<P>It do look tight though!<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||02-03-2000||05:44 PM||||I just dug the instructions out of one of my binders. I printed out a copy of the e-mail. I don't have the time to type it out right now, but will have it posted in several hours.||||reg||10
Z||000003||fiero56||02-03-2000||06:20 PM||||Looks like I have a little more time than I thought, here are the instructions:<BR>1) Remove 5 (10mm?) bolts holding top of fender.<BR>2) Remove all the wheel to fender bolts (7mm?).<BR>3) It is not necessary to remove the rivet in the rocker panel.<BR>4) Unscrew antenna.<BR>5) Looking at where the antennas base is located in the frame on the outboard horizontal ridge, about 1" back and 0.5" outboard from antenna location.<BR>You'll see that you have enough verticle room underneath between the wheelwell and door to fit the motor so that the antenna comes out verticle.<BR>6) Drill a verticle hole in the ridge (there are 2 layers of steel).<BR>7) Insert antenna through the hole, and pass the wires where the antenna coax comes out from the inside of the car (discard old antenna coax).<BR>8) Attach antenna on top and on the bottom with the hardware that should be included with it.<BR>9) Drill a 3/8" hole in the fender 1" back and 0.5" outboard from the original hole. Use the grommet from the antenna hole to make it look good.<BR>10) Reinstall fender.<BR>11) Remove plastic cover inside of car, so you can pull the carpet away and fish out the wires.<BR>12) Connect antenna coax to radio.<BR>13) I did not wire the power wires to the radio, I use a switch between my window actuators instead as I want the antenna in when I listen to cassttes.<BR>14) Reinstall fender and bolt everything together.<P>I have not done this to my car yet, but is something I may try later on.<BR>The guy who came up with this trick is Bert Sager, and his e-mail is: bert_sager@hotmail.com ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-03-2000||08:09 PM||||Drill a hole?  In my Fiero?  Are you nuts?!  I just might try this in the spring on my '85 GT.  I also remember a thread about installing one on the rear somewhere.  I'd do a search but I'm just too tired tonight.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||02-04-2000||11:58 PM||||Fiero56, thanks for the information! I'll scope it out.<P>Terry||||reg||1
